  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 308 AM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nThe overall synoptic pattern in this forecast period features two \ndifferent setups. Guidance continues to outline a ridging pattern \ncontinuing through Wednesday; exhibiting high-pressure, dry air, and \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. A duo of shortwave troughs are expected to \npropagate through the system, the first of which early Tuesday and \nthe second one Wednesday. Weak low pressure is expected to develop \nat the surface as well with these features. However, no fronts or \nprecip is anticipated. Thursday through Friday is when ensemble \nguidance has begun to display a longwave trough developing and \nmoving across the southwestern CONUS. Said trough could become \nnegatively tilted and eject a jet streak through the County Warning \nArea (CWA). This would likely boost surface winds for Friday. As \nthis deepening system makes its way across the western US, a strong \nsurface low is likely to develop in the northern Rockies, moving \neastward into the northern Plains late Thursday. Simultaneously, a \ncold front likely moves through the CWA late Friday evening.\n\nModel guidance is quite confident on Probability of Precipitation \n(PoPs) presenting less than 10% initially, which means very low \nchance of rainfall through Wednesday. Elevated PoP chances first \npresent around the evening timeframe Thursday. 20-35% is forecast \nright now but this could very well change as models get a better \ngrasp of the synoptic setup. Heading into Friday, Rainfall chances \nhave seen a bit of a boost from recent long-term forecasts, \nparticularity later on in the evening when the frontal passage is \nunderway. 25-35% PoPs early in the day should see an uptick to 30-\n50% through the evening hours. Dewpoints should range around the mid \nto upper 50s so there will likely be adequate moisture to work with. \nAt this time low confidence exists for any of this activity becoming \nconvective, given the projected low CAPE/low bulk shear environment \nbut will have to be monitored as we head into next week.\n\nA warming trend, with respect to high and low temperatures, should \npersist across the CWA and last through pre-frontal passage Friday. \nTuesday high temps consistently low/mid 70s should increase a bit to \nupper 70s/low 80s on Wednesday with subsidence, via the ridge, \ndominating the region. Highs are anticipated to peak on Thursday in \nthe low/mid 80s then settle around the upper 70s to low 80s range \nmid-afternoon before the cold front arrives. Overnight low temps are \nexpected to originally be cooler Tuesday/Wednesday mornings(40s-low \n50s) then become milder for Thursday/Friday with consistent 50s \nacross the entire CWA. \n\nFire weather will be monitored leading up to this four-day period.\nRelative humidity (RH) values in the 25-45% range are forecasted \neach day through Friday during peak heating late afternoon. \nWednesday will be the driest day as RH could dip into the 20s across \na good chunk of the CWA at or west of US-385. It will be quite humid \neach night as temperatures cool to the dew point. Moving onto \nsurface winds, Thursday and Friday are likely to be the windiest \ndays during this period and could see stronger 25-35 mph winds, \ngusting 40-45 mph in eastern Colorado. Gusts up to 30 mph are \ngenerally possible everywhere else on Thursday and Friday. At this \nmoment in time, no areas of critical fire weather are forecasted. \nNonetheless, elevated fire weather is still possible, especially \nareas west of the Colorado/Kansas state line where lower RH matches \nup with stronger winds.",
